\chapter{Analysis facts and equality saturation}
The compiler's declarative inputs live in \api{mull/rewrite}. Analysis files
end in \api{.a}; transformations end in \api{.t}. They use MuDSL's
S-expression reader, including semicolon comments, quoted atoms, and
\api{?variable} pattern bindings. There is no second rule parser.

\section{Facts are not executable alternatives}
\begin{rulecode}
(check lexical)
(check control-flow)
(rewrite binding-category
  (category (name ?text (names) ?site)) binding)
\end{rulecode}
Analysis policies activate scope and loop checks. For reference/assignment
targets, the pass inserts a \api{category} query into an analysis e-graph,
saturates the \api{.a} rules, and tests equivalence with \api{binding}.
This graph is distinct from the executable graph: treating a type fact as an
executable replacement would be incorrect. Additional shipped facts classify
number and string literals for consumers of the analysis vocabulary.

\section{Sound transformation rules}
\begin{rulecode}
(rewrite true-and
  (binary "and" (names) ?site
    (literal "true" (names) ?left) ?x)
  ?x)
\end{rulecode}
An e-class retains both expressions. Rebuilding propagates equality through
parents, and extraction chooses a finite representative with minimum node count.
The result evaluates \api{?x} once. By contrast, rewriting \api{x * 0} to zero
would suppress calls, undefined-name errors, and type errors. Reassociating
addition changes binary64 rounding. Neither rule is shipped.

The default rules simplify literal short circuits, literal negation, and unary
plus on numeric literals. They preserve operand-valued \api{and}/\api{or};
zero remains truthy. Source metadata is carried by matched variables or retained
on the replacement. Parsing does not evaluate numeric literals, so removing a
unary plus still leaves the literal's finite-number validation in place.

\section{Budgets, loading, and reproducibility}
Default limits are 12 saturation iterations, 20,000 e-nodes, and 20,000 matches.
\api{lastSaturation} records saturation or the limiting resource. Exhausting an
optimization budget still permits extracting an equivalent expression. An
incomplete analysis query is an error, rather than an assumed fact. These are
optimizer budgets, separate from the evaluator's step and frame limits; the
initial input graph is constructed before saturation applies its node budget.

\begin{dcode}
auto vm = new MuLL.Runtime;
vm.mullier.loadDirectory("mull/rewrite");
vm.mullier.limits.iterations = 8;
auto value = vm.run("return false or 42;");
assert(value.number() == 42);
\end{dcode}
Directory loading sorts filenames, requires both extensions, and replaces the
rules transactionally. Invalid syntax leaves the preceding rules installed.
Rule sets are trusted compiler extensions; an arbitrary equality is not proven
correct by saturation. Normal execution uses embedded rules generated by
\api{mull/rewrite/embed.py}, avoiding a working-directory dependency.
CTest checks the generated C++ and D rule snapshots for drift.
